<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaiah Berlin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Isaiah Berlin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was a British social and political theorist, philosopher and historian of ideas, born into a Russian-Jewish family in Riga in 1909. As a boy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> he witnessed the Russian Revolution, and in 1921 his family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. From 1957 to 1967 he was Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>; his inaugural lecture, Two Concepts of Liberty (1958), set negative liberty, the absence of interference, against positive liberty, self-mastery. He became the founding president of Wolfson College, Oxford, in 1966, and his work on liberal theory and value pluralism, and his opposition to Marxism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, has had a lasting influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaiah-berlin.jpg" alt="Isaiah Berlin" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob C. Croes (ANEFO) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsaiahBerlin1983.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Isaiah Berlin</b>, in lectures given in 1965 and published as The Roots of Romanticism in 1999, called it the greatest single shift in the consciousness of the West.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the late 20th century liberals have been divided, with Michael Walzer, <b>Isaiah Berlin</b>, Charles Taylor and David Miller holding that a liberal society needs a stable nation-state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in Acland Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Riga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5070 231 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isaiah Berlin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
